<%# Faithful port of the legacy SPA `Layouts/navbar.js`:
    - brand → root
    - logged out: "Login" link (+ signup)
    - logged in: avatar menu (@handle, profile, notifications w/ unread indicator,
      "Log Out" via button_to DELETE /logout)
    - "New Claim" compose entry (compose form itself lands in Slice 2).

    Fixed, 56px, `bg-nav backdrop-blur` over a hairline (theme-aware — `bg-nav`/
    `border-hairline` retint with the active theme+scheme, Hoojah 2026 redesign Phase 0
    Task 0.4) — this bar is the ONLY blur in the product (docs/design-system/readme.md,
    VISUAL FOUNDATIONS). Its icons are 24px; 16px is for inline icons elsewhere.

    Every piece of text in here is `text-sm`. That is the design system's "UI text 14px",
    and it is stated on each element rather than once on the `<nav>` because the menu
    rows get theirs from `ds_menu_item_classes` and the `@handle` heading is a 12px
    micro-label — a single inherited size on the bar would have to be overridden twice
    and would read as the exception rather than the rule. Before Slice 9 these labels
    inherited 16px from the body, which was drift, not a decision.

    The icon links below are deliberately NOT `ds_button_classes`. That helper emits the
    house *button* — a pill, or the `link` variant used by the bare text controls in a
    notification row — and both carry `active:scale-95`, which the design system assigns
    to vote buttons, not to chrome. Navbar entries stay anchors, `New Claim` included.
    Trending is the one 2026 exception: it gets a filled `bg-card-2` icon-button LOOK
    (Task 0.4) without switching to `ds_button_classes` itself — same anchor, same lack
    of `active:scale-95`, just a coloured box instead of a bare icon. %>
<nav class="fixed top-0 inset-x-0 z-20 bg-nav backdrop-blur border-b border-hairline">
  <%# Three-zone bar (owner-approved). The inner bar matches the page body's
      container exactly (`max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4`, same as feed index's <main>), so the
      left/right zones align with the body content edges instead of the viewport edges.
      The logo is CENTRED at every width now: source order is already LEFT → logo → RIGHT,
      so `justify-between` (<sm) spreads the three, and the 3-column grid (>=sm) with
      `justify-self-*` pins each zone (left / centre / right) so the logo is TRULY centred
      regardless of side widths. No `order-*` overrides — the DOM order IS the visual order.
      LEFT  = user dropdown (signed-in only) + theme/scheme pill ("accessibility tools")
      CENTER = brand (the "h" MARK below sm, the full wordmark at >=sm)
      RIGHT = Login/Sign up (guest) · Trending (mobile/tablet only) · New Claim (signed-in) %>
  <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 h-14 flex items-center justify-between sm:grid sm:grid-cols-3">
    <%# LEFT zone %>
    <div class="sm:justify-self-start flex items-center gap-3">
      <% if user_signed_in? %>
        <%# `<details>` is the menu mechanism (design system: components/navigation/
            DropdownMenu.prompt.md). The `dropdown` controller adds dismiss-on-outside-click
            + Escape to match every other menu in the app; the JS-off contract is unchanged
            (the <summary> still toggles it natively). %>
        <details class="relative" data-controller="dropdown">
          <summary class="list-none cursor-pointer flex items-center">
            <%# 36px, the design system's `nav` avatar box. Via the partial rather than
                `image_tag current_user.photo`, which raises on a user whose photo is
                blank — reachable, since `:photo` is permitted in `user_params` and
                `assign_random_photo` only backfills on create. That would 500 the
                navbar, i.e. every signed-in page at once.

                `variant: :tile` (Hoojah 2026, Task 0.4): the gradient-initials TREATMENT
                on purpose, not merely the no-photo fallback — every signed-in user gets
                the tile here, photo or not.

                Extracted into `shared/nav_current_user_avatar` (Issue #13) so a successful
                profile update can Turbo-replace it live via update.turbo_stream.erb — the
                partial wraps the tile in a stable `nav_avatar` target. The unread dot below
                stays OUTSIDE that span (it tracks unread count, not the profile edit). %>
            <%= render "shared/nav_current_user_avatar", user: current_user %>
            <% if current_user.unread_notifications_count > 0 %>
              <%# 8px pink dot, never a count badge — the design system is explicit.
                  `role="img"` for the same reason `ui/_avatar`'s fallback carries one:
                  ARIA prohibits an author-supplied name on `role=generic`, which a bare
                  <span> is, so without it a screen reader may drop the label entirely
                  and the only unread signal left is a pink circle. `img` and not
                  `status`: a live region announces *changes*, and this dot is rendered
                  on page load rather than streamed in, so `status` would re-announce on
                  every navigation and never fire when it was supposed to. %>
              <span role="img" aria-label="Unread notifications"
                    class="ml-1 inline-block w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-neutral"></span>
            <% end %>
            <%# L/XL only: the @handle beside the avatar (navbar spec — "Avatar + Username").
                Hidden below lg, where the bar is tight and the avatar alone is the user menu.
                `truncate`+`max-w` so a long username can't widen the left zone into the
                centred brand. `pointer-events-none` so the whole summary stays the click
                target (the text isn't a separate hit area that could feel like a link). %>
            <span class="hidden lg:block ml-2 max-w-[9rem] truncate text-sm font-semibold text-ink pointer-events-none">@<%= current_user.username %></span>
          </summary>
          <%# align: "left-0" — the avatar is the LEFT-most nav item now, so its panel
              opens rightward (left edge under the trigger), not off the viewport left. %>
          <%= render layout: "ui/menu", locals: {width: "w-56", align: "left-0"} do %>
            <h6 class="text-xs text-grey px-3 pb-1">@<%= current_user.username %></h6>
            <%= link_to "Your profile", profile_path(current_user.username), class: ds_menu_item_classes %>
            <%= link_to "Dashboard", dashboard_path, class: ds_menu_item_classes %>
            <%# Pending follow-requests inbox (2026 follow gaps). Like the moderation row
                below, the count is a text suffix in the menu row, not a bar badge — the
                navbar's "8px dot, never a count" rule governs the BAR itself; a menu row
                may state a number in words. One indexed COUNT on follows.followed_id per
                signed-in render, same class of query as unread_notifications_count. %>
            <%= link_to follow_requests_path, class: ds_menu_item_classes do %>
              Follow requests
              <% pending = current_user.passive_follows.pending.count %>
              <% if pending > 0 %>
                <span id="nav-follow-requests-count" class="ml-1 text-xs text-grey">(<%= pending %>)</span>
              <% end %>
            <% end %>
            <%# Staff-only review queue (2026 moderation). Gated on can_moderate? so the
                pending_moderation_count query runs only for moderators/admins — an ordinary
                page load never pays for it. The count is a text suffix in the row, not a
                bar badge: the navbar's "8px dot, never a count" rule governs the bar itself,
                and a menu row may state a number in words. %>
            <% if current_user.can_moderate? %>
              <%= link_to moderation_path, class: ds_menu_item_classes do %>
                Moderation
                <% count = pending_moderation_count %>
                <% if count > 0 %>
                  <span id="nav-moderation-count" class="ml-1 text-xs text-grey">(<%= count %>)</span>
                <% end %>
              <% end %>
              <%# Admin listings (Issue #18) — read-only all-users / all-hoojahs indexes.
                  Same staff gate as the Moderation row; plain rows, no count, no dot. %>
              <%= link_to "All users", admin_users_path, class: ds_menu_item_classes %>
              <%= link_to "All hoojahs", admin_hujahs_path, class: ds_menu_item_classes %>
            <% end %>
            <%= link_to notifications_path, class: ds_menu_item_classes do %>
              Your notifications
              <%# The same dot, unlabelled and hidden: the row it sits in already says
                  "Your notifications", so a label here would announce the row twice. %>
              <% if current_user.unread_notifications_count > 0 %>
                <span aria-hidden="true" class="ml-1 inline-block w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-neutral align-middle"></span>
              <% end %>
            <% end %>
            <%= render "ui/divider", class: "my-1" %>
            <%# `button_to` emits its own form and button, so the class string is the
                only seam it has — see the note on `ds_menu_item_classes`. %>
            <%= button_to "Log Out", destroy_user_session_path, method: :delete,
                  class: ds_menu_item_classes %>
          <% end %>
        </details>
      <% end %>

      <%# Theme + scheme control ("accessibility tools", Hoojah 2026). A compact pill,
          reachable on every page since the navbar is shared. Both controls fail safe with
          JS off: the server renders the light+spectrum defaults and the page is fully
          usable; this only flips <html>'s data-theme / data-scheme and persists to
          localStorage (the no-FOUC head script re-applies before paint). Uses the
          theme-aware `bg-card` / `border-hairline` / `text-ink` utilities so the pill
          retints with the active theme. It sits AFTER the user menu so the left zone reads
          "user menu · accessibility tools" left-to-right (a guest has only this pill). %>
      <div class="flex items-center rounded-full border border-hairline bg-card" data-controller="theme">
        <button type="button" data-action="theme#toggleTheme" data-theme-target="toggle"
                class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 text-ink" aria-label="Toggle dark mode">
          <%= lucide_icon("sun-moon", class: "w-4 h-4") %>
        </button>
        <button type="button" data-action="theme#cycleScheme" data-theme-target="scheme"
                class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 text-ink" aria-label="Change colour scheme">
          <%= lucide_icon("palette", class: "w-4 h-4") %>
        </button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <%# CENTER zone — brand, centred at every width (grid `justify-self-center` at >=sm;
        `justify-between` spreads it to the middle below sm). Two glyphs, one visible per
        breakpoint: the compact "h" MARK below sm (where the full wordmark would crowd the
        user menu + actions on the narrowest phones), the full wordmark from sm up. Both are
        `h-7 w-auto` and share the same 0..28 vertical box, so the "h" lands at the exact
        height the wordmark's own "h" would. The link's aria-label names the brand, so the
        images stay decorative (empty alt) rather than announcing "Hoojah" twice. %>
    <%= link_to root_path, class: "sm:justify-self-center flex items-center no-underline", aria: { label: "Hoojah" } do %>
      <%= image_tag "logo-mark.svg", alt: "", class: "h-7 w-auto sm:hidden" %>
      <%= image_tag "logo.svg", alt: "", class: "h-7 w-auto hidden sm:block" %>
    <% end %>

    <%# RIGHT zone — reads Login/Sign up · Trending · New Claim left-to-right. The auth
        links (guest) and New Claim (signed-in) are mutually exclusive, so the row is
        auth-links → Trending → New: a guest sees "Login  Sign up  🔥", a signed-in user
        sees "🔥  New". %>
    <div class="sm:justify-self-end flex items-center gap-3">
      <% unless user_signed_in? %>
        <%= link_to "Login", new_user_session_path, class: "text-sm text-primary no-underline" %>
        <%= link_to "Sign up", new_user_registration_path, class: "text-sm text-grey no-underline" %>
      <% end %>

      <%# Hoojah 2026: a filled icon-button box (bg-card-2, ~38px), not a bare icon link —
          see the note above about why every OTHER navbar entry stays a plain anchor;
          trending is the one exception the 2026 mockup gives a button treatment. %>
      <%= link_to trending_path, class: "lg:hidden flex items-center justify-center gap-1 h-10 px-2 bg-card-2 text-neutral rounded-xl no-underline", aria: {label: "Trending"} do %>
        <%= lucide_icon("flame", class: "w-5 h-5") %>
        <%# Label from md up only (XS/S are icon-only per the navbar spec). The button
            itself is `lg:hidden`, so the label is effectively visible at md alone. %>
        <span class="text-sm hidden md:inline">Trending</span>
      <% end %>

      <% if user_signed_in? %>
        <%= link_to new_hujah_path, class: "flex items-center text-primary no-underline", aria: { label: "New Claim" } do %>
          <%= lucide_icon("message-square-plus", class: "w-6 h-6") %>
          <span class="hidden lg:inline ml-1 text-sm">New Claim</span>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </div>
</nav>
<div class="h-14"></div>
